1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,810
Research Computing on Explorer and AICR. Installing Software on an HPC Cluster. How

2
00:00:04,830 --> 00:00:10,010
to get software running on a shared cluster without root: modules, Conda, Spack and

3
00:00:10,030 --> 00:00:14,468
EasyBuild, source builds, and Apptainer containers, and which one to reach for

4
00:00:14,488 --> 00:00:14,840
first.

5
00:00:14,910 --> 00:00:19,719
Jambo. This is Liam, in for Bear. First, a correction. On a shared cluster you do

6
00:00:19,739 --> 00:00:24,850
not install software the way you do on a laptop. There is no sudo, no root, no

7
00:00:24,870 --> 00:00:28,773
system package manager you are allowed to touch. Everything you install goes into

8
00:00:28,793 --> 00:00:32,697
your own space, built against the cluster's own libraries. This lecture walks the

9
00:00:32,717 --> 00:00:36,620
main ways to get research software running on a shared cluster: environment modules,

10
00:00:36,640 --> 00:00:40,242
Conda, Spack, EasyBuild, compiling from source with your own module files, and

11
00:00:40,262 --> 00:00:43,260
App-tainer containers. And then, which one to reach for first.

12
00:00:43,330 --> 00:00:47,395
Six terms. Root: the administrator account that can write to system directories; you

13
00:00:47,415 --> 00:00:52,109
do not have it. A module: a package an administrator already built, loaded by name

14
00:00:52,129 --> 00:00:56,194
and version. User space: your home, scratch or project directories, the only places

15
00:00:56,214 --> 00:01:00,594
you can install. A build from source: compiling a program's code yourself, with your

16
00:01:00,614 --> 00:01:05,308
own options. A module file: the small text file that tells the module command what

17
00:01:05,328 --> 00:01:09,393
to change. And a container: an application packed with its whole operating system

18
00:01:09,413 --> 00:01:10,650
into one image file.

19
00:01:10,720 --> 00:01:15,378
Why it is different. Your laptop: root access, one user, a local disk with no quota,

20
00:01:15,398 --> 00:01:19,179
and generic downloads that just work. The cluster: user space only, hundreds of

21
00:01:19,199 --> 00:01:22,979
people sharing the login node, a network filesystem with tight quotas, and builds

22
00:01:22,999 --> 00:01:27,365
that should link against the cluster's own compilers, M P I and math libraries. A

23
00:01:27,385 --> 00:01:32,043
generic binary from the internet ignores all of that, so it runs slower, or will not

24
00:01:32,063 --> 00:01:32,920
launch across nodes.

25
00:01:32,990 --> 00:01:37,383
Two habits follow. First, do not build on the login node. Everyone who is connected

26
00:01:37,403 --> 00:01:41,796
shares it; a long compile or a memory-hungry install there slows the system for all

27
00:01:41,816 --> 00:01:46,504
of them. Heavy builds go into an interactive job or a batch job. Second, mind the

28
00:01:46,524 --> 00:01:51,506
filesystem. Your home directory is a network filesystem, N F S, G P F S or Lustre,

29
00:01:51,526 --> 00:01:56,213
shared across every node. Quotas are tight, and it is tuned for large reads, not the

30
00:01:56,233 --> 00:02:00,920
tens of thousands of tiny files a Conda environment or a build tree creates. That is

31
00:02:00,940 --> 00:02:02,980
a real reason job launches are slow.

32
00:02:03,050 --> 00:02:06,636
Before installing anything, check whether it already exists. Most clusters expose

33
00:02:06,656 --> 00:02:10,569
preinstalled software through environment modules. This cluster uses the classic T C

34
00:02:10,589 --> 00:02:15,158
L based Environment Modules, not L mod, so a few commands differ from other

35
00:02:15,178 --> 00:02:19,747
tutorials. Module avail lists what is there; avail with a name filters it; show

36
00:02:19,767 --> 00:02:24,663
prints what a module would change; load adds it; list shows what is loaded; unload

37
00:02:24,683 --> 00:02:28,269
removes one; purge clears everything. Names and versions are cluster-specific, so

38
00:02:28,289 --> 00:02:29,580
always check avail first.

39
00:02:29,650 --> 00:02:33,424
What module load actually does. It does not install anything. It edits your shell's

40
00:02:33,444 --> 00:02:37,217
environment, the PATH variable and its library cousin, so the shell can find a

41
00:02:37,237 --> 00:02:41,011
package an administrator built once and shares with everyone. Load a module and PATH

42
00:02:41,031 --> 00:02:45,076
gains the package directory in front of user bin. Unload it and the directory is

43
00:02:45,096 --> 00:02:48,869
gone. That is how you switch between versions cleanly, with no conflicts. And the

44
00:02:48,889 --> 00:02:51,850
fastest install is the one you do not have to do.

45
00:02:51,920 --> 00:02:56,681
Conda is where most researchers start, especially in Python and R. It installs into

46
00:02:56,701 --> 00:03:01,121
your own directory, handles non-Python libraries too, and needs no root. On most

47
00:03:01,141 --> 00:03:05,560
clusters you load a Conda module rather than installing Conda yourself. Create an

48
00:03:05,580 --> 00:03:09,659
environment with a Python version and packages, activate it with source activate,

49
00:03:09,679 --> 00:03:13,757
and check. Channels: prefer conda-forge, which has far broader and more current

50
00:03:13,777 --> 00:03:17,172
coverage, and remove the defaults channel. Anaconda's terms restrict large

51
00:03:17,192 --> 00:03:21,270
organisations' use of its default channels, and many universities now avoid them.

52
00:03:21,340 --> 00:03:25,875
Now the pitfalls, the part people skip and then file a ticket about. Solving an

53
00:03:25,895 --> 00:03:30,733
environment is heavy, so do it in an interactive job, not on the login node. Watch

54
00:03:30,753 --> 00:03:35,592
your home quota: an environment is tens of thousands of small files. But do not put

55
00:03:35,612 --> 00:03:38,932
environments on scratch, because scratch is purged, and the environment vanishes,

56
00:03:38,952 --> 00:03:42,272
sometimes mid-project. Keep environments in persistent space, home or a project

57
00:03:42,292 --> 00:03:46,827
directory. The one piece you can safely send to scratch is the package cache, which

58
00:03:46,847 --> 00:03:49,560
is regenerable. Or just empty it now and then.

59
00:03:49,630 --> 00:03:54,763
In your dot conda R C, redirect only the package cache to scratch. If home is tight,

60
00:03:54,783 --> 00:03:59,007
create environments at a path in a project directory with dash p. And activate

61
00:03:59,027 --> 00:04:03,858
inside the job script, never in dot bash R C. Conda's init command writes a block

62
00:04:03,878 --> 00:04:08,708
into dot bash R C that auto-activates on every login; it slows every login and can

63
00:04:08,728 --> 00:04:10,830
break batch jobs. Comment that block out.

64
00:04:10,900 --> 00:04:15,672
The job script does the activating: load the Conda module, source activate, run. And

65
00:04:15,692 --> 00:04:20,806
for reproducibility, export the environment to a Y M L file, exact, with builds, and

66
00:04:20,826 --> 00:04:22,860
recreate it later from that file.

67
00:04:22,930 --> 00:04:27,521
Spack is a package manager built for HPC. Unlike Conda, it builds from source by

68
00:04:27,541 --> 00:04:32,439
default, and it lets you choose the compiler, the M P I implementation and the build

69
00:04:32,459 --> 00:04:36,435
options, holding many versions side by side. One honest note first. Spack and

70
00:04:36,455 --> 00:04:40,431
EasyBuild are heavy: long compiles, big disk footprints, real upkeep. They are not

71
00:04:40,451 --> 00:04:45,349
the first thing to reach for. If a module or Conda covers you, use that. Spack's

72
00:04:45,369 --> 00:04:49,960
core idea is the spec: package, at version, percent compiler, plus variant. H D F

73
00:04:49,980 --> 00:04:55,492
five at one point fourteen point three, percent G C C twelve point two, plus M P I.

74
00:04:55,512 --> 00:04:59,488
Spack resolves the whole dependency tree and builds each piece with exactly those

75
00:04:59,508 --> 00:05:01,640
choices. The version numbers here are illustrative.

76
00:05:01,710 --> 00:05:06,004
The workflow. Clone Spack, source its setup script to load it into your shell, list

77
00:05:06,024 --> 00:05:10,319
and info a package to see versions and variants, install it with the variant you

78
00:05:10,339 --> 00:05:14,633
want, and spack load it. A clean install with many dependencies compiles for a long

79
00:05:14,653 --> 00:05:18,660
time, so run spack install inside a job with several cores. A Spack environment

80
00:05:18,680 --> 00:05:22,399
groups specs for one project: create it, activate it, add specs, install everything,

81
00:05:22,419 --> 00:05:24,700
and commit the spack dot yaml it writes.

82
00:05:24,770 --> 00:05:28,292
EasyBuild solves a similar problem with a different philosophy. It builds from

83
00:05:28,312 --> 00:05:32,130
recipe files called easyconfigs, and you almost never write one: thousands ship with

84
00:05:32,150 --> 00:05:36,853
it, so you install a package by name and the recipe is fixed, which is the

85
00:05:36,873 --> 00:05:41,281
reproducibility selling point. A toolchain is a named bundle of compiler, M P I and

86
00:05:41,301 --> 00:05:46,595
math libraries; gom-pee is G C C plus Open M P I. There is no EasyBuild module here,

87
00:05:46,615 --> 00:05:51,613
so you install it once into a Conda environment. Then search for a recipe and run E

88
00:05:51,633 --> 00:05:54,270
B with three flags that are easy to miss.

89
00:05:54,340 --> 00:05:57,482
Those three flags. Modules tool equals Environment Modules, because EasyBuild

90
00:05:57,502 --> 00:06:02,856
assumes L mod by default and this cluster does not run it. Module syntax equals T C

91
00:06:02,876 --> 00:06:07,599
L, because Loo-ah module files will not load here. And install path in your own

92
00:06:07,619 --> 00:06:12,025
space, because you cannot write to system directories. Put them in a config file

93
00:06:12,045 --> 00:06:16,768
once. Then the key detail: EasyBuild's output is a module file. Point module at its

94
00:06:16,788 --> 00:06:20,878
directory with module use, and load the result with module load like any

95
00:06:20,898 --> 00:06:21,510
preinstalled package.

96
00:06:21,580 --> 00:06:26,651
Spack versus EasyBuild, in four rows. How you install: a spec on the command line,

97
00:06:26,671 --> 00:06:30,385
versus a named recipe. Dependencies: Spack resolves a flexible graph; EasyBuild

98
00:06:30,405 --> 00:06:35,137
follows a fixed toolchain. Output: spack load or a module, versus always a module

99
00:06:35,157 --> 00:06:38,191
file. Strength: flexible variants and versions, versus strict, repeatable,

100
00:06:38,211 --> 00:06:41,925
toolchain-based builds. And the candid note from experience: neither is the

101
00:06:41,945 --> 00:06:47,016
recommended first option on this cluster. Both are heavy to set up and painful to

102
00:06:47,036 --> 00:06:51,429
maintain, and Spack in particular can be frustrating when builds break. Reach for

103
00:06:51,449 --> 00:06:56,181
them only when you need source-level control and have decided the upkeep is worth

104
00:06:56,201 --> 00:06:56,520
it.

105
00:06:56,590 --> 00:07:01,090
Sometimes nothing prebuilt exists, or you need a patch, a specific version, or a

106
00:07:01,110 --> 00:07:05,288
flag no package manager exposes. Then you compile from source. The project's README

107
00:07:05,308 --> 00:07:10,454
or INSTALL file names its build system; the two you will meet most are Autotools, a

108
00:07:10,474 --> 00:07:14,651
configure script, and C make. Autotools: configure with a prefix, make with several

109
00:07:14,671 --> 00:07:19,171
cores, make install. C make: load the C make module, configure with an install

110
00:07:19,191 --> 00:07:22,400
prefix, build, install. Always read the project's own instructions first.

111
00:07:22,470 --> 00:07:27,566
What makes it an HPC build. Always set a prefix; you cannot install to the system's

112
00:07:27,586 --> 00:07:31,402
user local directory. Load the cluster's compiler and libraries as modules before

113
00:07:31,422 --> 00:07:35,879
you configure, so the build links against the optimised versions; that is the same

114
00:07:35,899 --> 00:07:39,715
reason Spack and EasyBuild produce fast binaries. Point parallel or numerical code

115
00:07:39,735 --> 00:07:45,470
at the cluster's M P I and BLAS and LAPACK, not a slow generic fallback. And run the

116
00:07:45,490 --> 00:07:50,266
make step inside a job, because dash j eight is a multi-core workload. Five steps:

117
00:07:50,286 --> 00:07:54,423
load modules, configure with a prefix, compile inside a job, install into the

118
00:07:54,443 --> 00:07:57,620
prefix, expose it on PATH or with a module file.

119
00:07:57,690 --> 00:08:01,726
After installing to a prefix, the shell still does not know the software exists.

120
00:08:01,746 --> 00:08:06,362
Export PATH and L D library path by hand, and it works for this session. For

121
00:08:06,382 --> 00:08:10,128
something you reuse, write your own module file instead. Make a directory, tell

122
00:08:10,148 --> 00:08:15,053
module to look there with module use, in your dot bash R C, so it persists, and

123
00:08:15,073 --> 00:08:19,689
write the file. This cluster's module files are T C L. The first line, hash percent

124
00:08:19,709 --> 00:08:24,324
Module, must be exactly that, the magic cookie that marks the file as a module file.

125
00:08:24,344 --> 00:08:28,670
Two prepend-path lines, one for PATH and one for the library path, and a one-line

126
00:08:28,690 --> 00:08:28,960
description.

127
00:08:29,030 --> 00:08:33,206
The directory name becomes the module name and the file name becomes the version.

128
00:08:33,226 --> 00:08:37,701
Now module avail shows your build, module load sets the paths for you, and unload

129
00:08:37,721 --> 00:08:41,597
reverses everything cleanly. One module file becomes a personal stack: each tool its

130
00:08:41,617 --> 00:08:46,392
own subdirectory, each version a file inside it, so my tool one point oh and two

131
00:08:46,412 --> 00:08:51,187
point oh sit side by side and you switch with one load, exactly like the cluster's

132
00:08:51,207 --> 00:08:56,582
compilers. A module file can load other modules, such as the right G C C, and a dot

133
00:08:56,602 --> 00:08:58,080
version file names the default.

134
00:08:58,150 --> 00:09:02,066
App tainer, formerly Singularity, brings containers to HPC. A container packs an

135
00:09:02,086 --> 00:09:06,330
application with its entire operating system environment into a single image file, a

136
00:09:06,350 --> 00:09:11,906
dot S I F, so it runs the same way everywhere. Why not Docker? Docker normally needs

137
00:09:11,926 --> 00:09:16,826
root to run, a security problem on a shared system. App-tainer runs a container as

138
00:09:16,846 --> 00:09:20,762
you, with no elevated privileges, works with the scheduler and the shared

139
00:09:20,782 --> 00:09:26,338
filesystem, runs G P U and M P I workloads, and can pull and convert Docker images,

140
00:09:26,358 --> 00:09:29,290
so you are not locked out of that ecosystem.

141
00:09:29,360 --> 00:09:33,008
App-tainer is installed system-wide, no module needed. Pull an existing image,

142
00:09:33,028 --> 00:09:38,010
including from Docker registries, into a dot S I F file. Exec runs one command

143
00:09:38,030 --> 00:09:43,679
inside it. Shell opens an interactive shell inside it. The dot S I F is one file:

144
00:09:43,699 --> 00:09:47,680
copy it, archive it, share it, and it reproduces the same environment.

145
00:09:47,750 --> 00:09:52,510
To build your own, you describe the image in a definition file: bootstrap from a

146
00:09:52,530 --> 00:09:56,653
Docker image, Ubuntu twenty-two oh four; a post section that installs Python and

147
00:09:56,673 --> 00:09:59,840
numpy; and a runscript that says what runs by default.

148
00:09:59,910 --> 00:10:04,807
Build images on your own machine, not on the cluster: building a dot S I F from

149
00:10:04,827 --> 00:10:08,856
scratch generally needs root. So build where you have it, your laptop or a

150
00:10:08,876 --> 00:10:13,772
workstation, then copy the finished image up with S C P and exec it on the cluster.

151
00:10:13,792 --> 00:10:17,532
The privileged step stays on a machine you control; only the unprivileged run

152
00:10:17,552 --> 00:10:21,292
happens here. Containers shine when software is hard to install natively, when a

153
00:10:21,312 --> 00:10:25,341
research group already publishes an image, or when you want one portable artifact to

154
00:10:25,361 --> 00:10:29,390
move between clusters or cite. The cost is image size and the upfront build.

155
00:10:29,460 --> 00:10:33,391
Choosing the tool. Six options, and none needs root. Environment modules:

156
00:10:33,411 --> 00:10:37,700
preinstalled, best for what the cluster already provides, limited to what admins

157
00:10:37,720 --> 00:10:42,369
installed. Conda: prebuilt binaries, best for Python and R and quick setups, heavy

158
00:10:42,389 --> 00:10:46,679
on quota. Spack: builds from source, source-level control and many variants, heavy

159
00:10:46,699 --> 00:10:50,630
and slow to maintain. EasyBuild: from source, reproducible toolchain builds, mostly

160
00:10:50,650 --> 00:10:54,940
an admin tool, toolchain-bound. A source install: full control, patches and niche

161
00:10:54,960 --> 00:11:00,327
tools, the most manual and the easiest to get wrong. App-tainer: an image, for hard

162
00:11:00,347 --> 00:11:03,200
dependencies and portability, built locally then copied up.

163
00:11:03,270 --> 00:11:07,847
Now, the practical order. One: check for a module first; the best install is no

164
00:11:07,867 --> 00:11:11,832
install. Two: a Python or R package? Conda, with the quota and login-node

165
00:11:11,852 --> 00:11:15,203
precautions. Three: isolated, portable, or a nightmare of dependencies? Build a

166
00:11:15,223 --> 00:11:20,107
container on your own machine and run it here. Four: a patch, a specific version, or

167
00:11:20,127 --> 00:11:23,785
a build flag nothing else exposes? Build from source. Five: source-level control

168
00:11:23,805 --> 00:11:28,689
over compiler, M P I or variants, and willing to maintain it? Then, and mostly then,

169
00:11:28,709 --> 00:11:32,980
Spack or EasyBuild. And if none of that fits, ask the Research Computing team.

170
00:11:33,050 --> 00:11:37,654
Let's recap with Claude. No root and a shared filesystem, so every install goes in

171
00:11:37,674 --> 00:11:42,277
your own space, off the login node, mindful of quota. Check for a module first;

172
00:11:42,297 --> 00:11:45,976
module load only edits your environment. Conda for Python and R: conda-forge,

173
00:11:45,996 --> 00:11:49,675
environments in persistent space, activate in the job script. Spack and EasyBuild

174
00:11:49,695 --> 00:11:53,990
build from source and are the heavy, advanced option, not the default. From source:

175
00:11:54,010 --> 00:11:58,614
a prefix, the cluster's modules and libraries, compile inside a job, and your own T

176
00:11:58,634 --> 00:12:04,470
C L module file. App-tainer: build the dot S I F locally, copy it up, run it as you.

177
00:12:04,540 --> 00:12:09,299
Your turn. Paste this into Claude: I need to run [name the software] on a shared HPC

178
00:12:09,319 --> 00:12:13,797
cluster where I have no root access and a tight home quota. Walk me down the

179
00:12:13,817 --> 00:12:18,014
decision order: is it likely a module, a Conda package, a container image, a source

180
00:12:18,034 --> 00:12:21,950
build, or a Spack/EasyBuild job? Give me the exact commands for the first option

181
00:12:21,970 --> 00:12:26,729
that fits, where each file should live, and what I must not run on the login node.

182
00:12:26,749 --> 00:12:30,946
Then check two things yourself. Run module avail for the name before you believe any

183
00:12:30,966 --> 00:12:35,444
install is needed. And confirm every path it gave you is in your own space, never

184
00:12:35,464 --> 00:12:36,850
the system's user local directory.

185
00:12:36,920 --> 00:12:42,290
Installing Software on an HPC Cluster. At Nik Bear Brown.
